# Working in this repository

This project is checked by [speclink](https://github.com/worldiety/speclink),
which links every requirement to the code that satisfies it and the test that
verifies it. The rules below are not style preferences: speclink enforces them,
and a change that breaks one fails the check with a message naming the fix.

## The loop

Run all of it before calling a change finished. Every step must end clean.

    go mod tidy
    go build ./...
    go test -json ./... | go run github.com/worldiety/speclink@latest evidence
    go run github.com/worldiety/speclink@latest verify

`evidence` records which tests verified which requirements. `verify` reports
everything else. **Every finding is an error** — there are no warnings, and
nothing is advisory.

On a fresh checkout, before any test has run, `verify` reports the requirements
as unverified and the stored shapes as unrecorded. That is correct rather than
broken: nothing has been tested and nothing has been approved yet. Running the
loop above once clears both.

When a stored shape changes on purpose, record the new one:

    go run github.com/worldiety/speclink@latest freeze

That writes `speclink.lock`, which is committed. Its diff is meant to be read:
it is where a change to something already persisted becomes visible.

## Where things go

    foundation/           the little framework this project has
    app/<context>/        one bounded context: model, ports, use cases
    app/<context>/rest/   HTTP presentation
    app/<context>/cli/    command line presentation
    app/<context>/adapter/<tech>/   implementations of the context's ports
    requirements/         requirements as Go values
    requirements/_sources/  the documents requirements cite
    cmd/<binary>/         the entry point, the only place naming an adapter

A context must not import another context, a presentation, or an adapter. It
declares ports; `cmd` decides what is behind them. This is what lets a test put
an in-memory implementation behind a port, and speclink checks it.

## Adding a use case

Four files, in this order.

1. **The requirement**, in `requirements/fun/<subject>/R-<ID>.spec.go`, citing a
   section of a document under `requirements/_sources/`. A requirement with no
   source is reported: it means somebody invented an obligation.

2. **The use case**, in `app/<context>/uc_<name>.go`. A function type named for
   what it does, with a `New<Type>` constructor taking the ports it needs. The
   first thing it does is audit its permission.

3. **The permission**, added to `app/<context>/perm.go` as a package level
   `Perm<Name>`, and the use case added to the `UseCases` bundle.

4. **The binding**, in `app/<context>/uc_<name>.annotation.go`, saying which
   requirement the use case satisfies. Bindings are ordinary Go, so a rename
   that forgets one does not compile.

Then a test that calls `spec.Verified(t, ...)` naming the requirement it proved.

## Requirements

Requirements are Go values, not prose in a wiki. Each has a stable `ID`, a
`Text` written with MUST or MAY, and `Sources` pointing at the document it came
from — an anchor into a Markdown file under `requirements/_sources/`, or
`Extern` for a standard or a law.

Do not edit `requirements/_sources/`. Those documents belong to whoever owns the
subject matter. If one changes, speclink reports which requirements depended on
the changed section, and each has to be looked at again.

Every section of a source document must be cited by some requirement, or marked
with `<!-- speclink:informative -->` on the line after its heading. That marker
says "this paragraph carries no obligation" and is a claim somebody makes, never
a guess the tool makes.

## When a rule is genuinely wrong

Waive it at the construct it applies to, with a reason:

    var _ = spec.For[Thing](
        spec.Waive("K14-REQ-UNVERIFIED", "why this one cannot be tested"),
    )

The reason is read by people and appears in the generated specification. A
waiver without a real reason is worse than the finding it silences.
